@inherits EmailPanelBase

<section class="help-section">
    <h2>Overview</h2>
    <p>
        DotNetCloud Email is a web-based email client. Add one or more accounts (IMAP/SMTP or Gmail), read
        and organise your messages, and compose mail from your browser.
    </p>
</section>

<section class="help-section">
    <h2>Accounts</h2>
    <p>
        Add an account with <strong>+ Account</strong> in the sidebar. Choose IMAP/SMTP and enter your
        server, port, username and password, or Gmail if your administrator configured Google OAuth.
    </p>
    <p>
        Switch accounts with the account selector. <strong>Sync now</strong> fetches new mail;
        <strong>Delete account</strong> removes the account and its synced messages.
    </p>
</section>

<section class="help-section">
    <h2>Composing</h2>
    <p>
        Compose opens the editor. Pick the account to send from, add recipients (Cc and Bcc are
        available), a subject and the message body.
    </p>
    <p>
        Attach files with the attachment picker, or copy one of your cloud files with
        <strong>Browse files</strong>. Attachments are staged on this module before the message is sent.
    </p>
</section>

<section class="help-section">
    <h2>Reading</h2>
    <p>
        Click a folder to list its threads, then a thread to open it. Message content is rendered in an
        isolated frame, so a message cannot run scripts or reach your session.
    </p>
    <p>Attachments download through a tab opened by DotNetCloud itself.</p>
</section>

<section class="help-section">
    <h2>Rules</h2>
    <p>
        Rules sort incoming mail automatically. Add conditions (from, subject, body, attachments) and
        actions (mark read, star, move, archive); rules run in priority order and can stop later rules.
    </p>
</section>

<ul class="help-tips">
    <li>Use rules to file newsletters automatically.</li>
    <li>Reply and Forward are in the thread header.</li>
    <li>Attachments are deduplicated, so the same file is stored once.</li>
</ul>
